    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Инспектор здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The inspector is here.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'inspector' straight to 'here'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I know.' Senya combines 'I' with 'know' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он хочет проверить кухню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He wants to check the kitchen.' Chef Viktor links 'he' directly with 'wants' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Пусть проверяет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let him check.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'let' with 'is testing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он ищет нелегалов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is looking for illegals.' Notice how 'he' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'undocumented worker'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "У нас их нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We do not have them.' To express possession, Russian uses the preposition 'by' plus the genitive pronoun followed by 'no'. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где Федя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where is Fedya?' Chef Viktor combines 'where' with 'Fedya' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Он в холодильнике.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is in the fridge.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'he' with 'fridge'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Отлично.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excellent.' Chef Viktor answers concisely with the word for 'perfect'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Виктор Петрович, будьте любезны.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Viktor Petrovich, would you be so kind.' Notice how 'Viktor' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'kind'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Откройте, пожалуйста, холодильник.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Open the fridge, please.' the inspector links 'open' directly with 'please' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Я жду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am waiting.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'I'm waiting'.",
